  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 113 AM CDT Tue Sep 1 2026\n\nTropical Storm Edouard off the central Louisiana Gulf Coast should \ncontinue west-northwest, making landfall over Texas by this evening. \nNo impacts are expected in our forecast area. An anomalously strong \nupper-level ridge over the Mid-South is at nearly max strength for \nearly September. Easterly flow around its southern perimeter will \nbring disturbances and rain chances along the Gulf Coast, with rain \nchances mainly shunted to the south of our area to our area through \ntomorrow. Thursday, an easterly disturbance lifting into our area \nwill weaken the influence of the ridge and arrive with a surge of \ndeeper tropical moisture. Rain chances will spread farther north \ninto our area then Thursday and Friday. By the weekend into early \nnext week, the ridge will retrograde and amplify over the High \nPlains and along the Rockies. Eventually this should translate to \nlower rain chances and strengthening heat from the ridge.\n\nApart from increasing rain chances later in the week, heat and \nhumidity will still result in afternoon heat index values climbing \ninto the 105 to 110 degree range for parts of the area. Today \nthere's enough confidence to support a Heat Advisory across most \nareas except eastern Mississippi. Additional Heat Advisories may be \nneeded throughout the week ahead. /NF/",
